      "heading": "Module Recap and Common Mistakes",
      "html": "<p><strong>Takeaways:</strong></p>\n<p><ul><li>Refrigerant loops: hot high-pressure gas → liquid in the condenser → low-pressure boil in the evaporator → gas back to the compressor.</li><li>Low pressure makes refrigerant boil cold; high pressure lets it condense outside.</li><li>R-134a and R-1234yf are different refrigerants — read the label, never mix them.</li><li>Air and moisture hurt cooling and corrode parts; evacuation removes them.</li><li>Gauge pressures are the cycle's vital signs.</li></ul></p>\n<p><strong>Common mistake:</strong> Topping up a system that has a leak instead of finding and fixing the leak first. Refrigerant is not used up in a healthy system — if it is low, it went somewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Ready-to-Work link:</strong> If you can say, for each part of the loop, whether the refrigerant is a gas or a liquid and whether it is hot or cold, you have this module.</p>\n"
